 about a  50/50 election and we were telling him  it's a landslide with only 5% of the  vote and they couldn't believe that you  could predict the thing as accurately as  we did which was when a few percent with  only 5% of the vote so everybody was  thrown into total confusion for the  Republic it was me people were shocked  and symbolically from that moment the  computer had truly arrived in the minds  of the people it could do anything  here's a 1960s East German film  depicting a future computer of 1987  films such as this captured the  imagination of anyone watching in the  day globally young tech minds would have  been entranced by the concept of a  computer growing up one of those young  minds was Bill Gates an in January of  1975 he saw a magazine cover that would  change his life and the course of  history that magazine was popular  electronics and on the cover of that  magazine was a personal computer in an  instant all the possibilities were now  within reach there'll be more on Bill  Gates later but first where did this  computer come from well one year earlier  in 1974 Edie Roberts an engineer was  trying to prevent his calculator  business from going under at a meeting  with his bank lenders in a last-ditch  attempt to save the company he said that  he could do something that hadn't been  done before create a personal computer  kit for hobbyists a task thought to be  impossible at the time and only made  possible by the microprocessor invented  by Intel just three years earlier in  1971 for a bit of context the  microprocessor for the first time  allowed an entire computer to be put  onto one chip a truly huge feat Roberts  name the microprocessor driven computer  the Altair 8800 and soldered in the  pages of the popular electronics  magazine for $400 in January of 1975  Edie Roberts told the bank that they  expected a few hundred orders of the  most so ended up being swamped with a  couple of thousand orders within the  first few months at that time all the  computers in the world tended to be in  big centers and you had to get  permission to get close to them and it  was you know you said nobody could  nobody had access to computers and the  idea that you could have your own  computer do whatever you wanted to with  it whenever you wanted to was fantastic  the demand for the computer was so high  that enthusiastic hobbyists parked  outside of the front of his warehouse to  get their hands on the computers the  enthusiasm was driven by the fact that  now everything that these guys had  dreamed of and seen in movies was now  within reach of everyone and moreover it  could fit on your desk  well almost you see the Altair 8800 was  a very primitive there was no place to  connect a keyboard or monitor it was  just a bunch of switches and lights  although very simple the Altair 8800  started a revolution in mid 1975 a club  was started in California in which  enthusiasts gathered to discuss  everything about the Altair 8800 the  club's name was the homebrew Computer  Club and one of the enthusiasts was a  guy named Steve Wozniak Wozniak later  stated that it would be the very first  meeting at the homebrew Computer Club  that inspired him to build his own  computer which he would bring to each  weekly meeting this computer eventually  became the Apple one the computer that  started Apple in addition to being  primitive the Altair 8800 was very  difficult to use to load an instruction  he had to flip switches for example  let's look at how you'd load two plus  two  each two took 8 different switches and a  knife switch to load them all the Plus  instruction took 8 more switches and you  would know that the answer was 4 when  the third light from the left turned on  a 100 byte program would take 100  switches it kind of makes me thankful  for the ease of use of computers today  despite the hurdles though the guys at  the computer Club was smart enough to  come up with uses for it  Steve Dampier set up an altar laborious  leaky to program him to it somebody  knocked a plug out of the wall and he  had to do that all over again but nobody  knew what this was about after all and  wasn't just going to sit and flash his  lights no you put a little up transistor  radio next to the L tear and he would  bike manipulating the length of loops in  the software could play tunes the radio  began playing the fool on the hill and  the tinny little tunes that you could  tell we're coming from the noise that  the computer was generated being picked  up by the radio everybody rose and  applauded it was a very exciting thing  it was probably the first thing the LT  air actually did although this  demonstration blew minds at the time in  order to be a useful tool the machine  needed a programming language as it  turns out Paul Allen a high school  friend of Bill Gates saw the magazine  cover with the 8800 proudly sitting on  it he snatched it off the shelf and ran  into bills college dorm room when Bill  saw it he knew that this was it the  personal computer had arrived but  instead of getting excited bill saw a  business opportunity and called up ed  Roberts his company met and told them  that he'd written some software for the  8800 and it was almost completed the  thing was they hadn't even started  writing it yet and saw this popular  electronics magazine and it was kind of  in a way you know good news bad news  here was someone making a computer  around this chip in exactly the way that  Paul had talked to me in  thought about what kind of software  could be done for it and it was  happening without us and for all we knew  maybe they had some software people they  were just going to go charge off and do  this thing because we never had the chip  just the book from Intel if we'd made  any mistake in terms of how the  instructions worked it never I think  never would have run and so Paul was  scheduled to fly out to Albuquerque he  decided to go get some sleep  I stayed up all night reading the book  to see if we miscoded some of the  instructions and finally decided it was  all okay punch out the paper tape and  made sure Paul got that before he went  off on his plane he wrote the bootstrap  loader that is the thing you have to key  in to make this computer smart enough to  know to go get data off the teletype to  read it into memory he wrote that on the  plane on the way out loaded up in the  paper tape the first time for some  reason it didn't work the second time  they loaded in and it worked so it came  up said ready and he typed in a program  you know print 2+2 it worked he had to  print out squares and sums and and  things like that and he and and Roberts  the end of his company sat there and  they were amazed by you know that this  thing worked I mean Paul was amazed that  that our part had worked and in ed was  amazed his hardware worked and this was  the first piece of Microsoft software  after this moment the 19 year old Bill  Gates saw that he could get an early  foothold on the new PC industry and  immediately quit University and went to  live in New Mexico out of a hotel  directly across the road from the mitts  factory the company that made the Altair  machine from this hotel bill and Paul  got to work writing more software for  the 8800 their software allowed the 8800  to now do much more by the end of the  year people had attached terminals to  the out air and wrote games accounting  software and word processors on top of  Bill and Allen software code now called  Microsoft the true revolution had begun  the simple computer with switches and  lights had now become a functioning  machine closer to what we call a PC  today just in the span of one  yeah and this was all thanks to the  software that these two kids wrote and  as we know the rest went pretty well for  Microsoft so there you have it the  Altair 8800 the little-known first  personal computer if it didn't exist  there would be no Microsoft or Apple as  we know them today or perhaps even at  all and as a side point I think it's  really cool that we can now just click a  mouse and tap on a screen to load some  software instead of flipping hundreds of  switches we should be grateful for that  anyway thanks for watching if you think  more people should learn about this  golden piece of history feel free to  share this video also give it a thumbs  up if you liked it or thumbs down if you  didn't if you want to see some more cool  stuff from this channel hang around for  the next 10 seconds and follow the  prompts in your screen this has been -  gogo  you've been watching ColdFusion cheers  guys and I'll catch you again soon for  the next video have a good one  ColdFusion  it's rethinking

